Parked on binding-field access in ARGUMENT position, and no longer on `~`.

This test reported SUCCESS for as long as the tilde wall has existed
(2026-07-24) while its entire output was the wall's own diagnostic:

    Error: `~` is not legal in interpreter source. ...

MUST_RUN with no expected.txt asserts only "exited 0", and the program handles
`parse-error` by printing it, so a benchmark that never once executed a flow
counted as a passing benchmark. Fixed 2026-08-07: the tilde is gone from
FLOW_SOURCE, and the flow named `print_result` while the scope registers
`print-result`, which was event-denied the moment the tilde stopped hiding it.

It now really runs. What it prints:

    Result: <r.value>

`compute` returns 59 and the `result` branch is taken, but the continuation
`print-result(r.value)` passes the literal placeholder text instead of the
bound field. Plain bindings work (430_035 pins `n: v` green); it is field
access on a binding in argument position that does not resolve. Same value
model as the f64 hole parked in 430_041 — the interpreter carries argument
values as strings and re-reads their type at the far end.

expected.txt asserts the arithmetic the benchmark exists to measure, so this
flips green when binding-field access lands.
